Peering into the Dark Wind, Magi learn to notice the patterns beneath ordinary
things. Heat has a shape. Distance can be folded. Memory leaves an untouched
copy of itself somewhere behind the mind. Even death and a stolen moment of
time can be written down, studied, and changed. A Mage keeps those discoveries
in a leather-bound spellbook and tests them where theory becomes real.

The first pages are deliberately practical. Mana Shard and Missile are reliable
attacks, while Analyze, Detect, and Blaze give a new Mage useful ways to examine
and move through the world. Fighting level-appropriate enemies supplies Mage
guild XP, called GXP. Useful field casting awards 10 GXP for a utility working
or 20 for a combat working, at most once per working every thirty seconds. As
that study raises Arcane rank, the spellbook reveals more ambitious workings.

Each working also remembers how often it has mattered. It begins Copied, then
becomes Practiced, Fluent, Inscribed, and finally Instinctive. Practice lowers
spell costs and opens rune sockets. Combat workings can become automatic
barrages at Practiced mastery, firing from the spellbook alongside ordinary
attacks while the Mage remains free to cast something else.

A FOCUS weapon turns that technique into the battlemage style. While one is
wielded, even a freshly Copied barrage working can fire automatically for no SP.
Wearing AURA equipment at the same time deepens the resonance, making those
casts faster and stronger. The same equipment also helps shorten Recollection,
the Arcane 3 working that restores every missing spell point after a brief,
vulnerable weave.

Investiture opens at Arcane 4 and borrows either half of that resonance for a
time. It can write FOCUS over an ordinary wielded weapon or surround the Mage
with a temporary AURA. Only one Investiture can be sustained, so it can complete
a real piece of battlemage equipment but cannot replace both.

Runes let two Magi take the same page in different directions. Seven patterns
arrive through ordinary Arcane study; the rest are earned through mastery,
elemental practice, and discoveries on the ruined Isle of the Magi. The oldest
pages there still carry the names Arcanarton and Bodach, while Trivlian's
green grimoire waits in the Darkwind attic.

